  "worked_solution": "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The apple falls for $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n- We can find the distance the apple falls in meters as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =0.08\\,\\text{km} \\\\  & =0.08\\,\\text{km}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\text{km}} \\\\  & =0.08\\,\\cancel{\\text{km}}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}} \\\\  & =0.08\\cdot 1\\,000\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =80\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $u,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ (80) & =u(4)+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(4)^{2} \\\\ 80 & =4u+78.4 \\\\ 1.6 & =4u \\\\ u & =0.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the initial velocity of the apple was $u=0.4\\,\\text{m/s}.$",
